1. Deep Context Understanding
A real developer understands why  code exists, not just what  it does. The tool needs to grasp:
 
Business logic and domain constraints
 
The broader architecture and how this piece fits
 
Historical decisions (tech debt, workarounds, intentional trade-offs)
 
Non-code context (ticket requirements, team conventions, stakeholder needs)
2. Intent Inference Over Pattern Matching
Humans infer intent  from ambiguous signals. The tool must:
 
Read between the lines of poorly named variables or rushed comments
 
Understand the goal even when the implementation is messy
 
Recognize when code is temporary vs. production-ready
 
Distinguish between "this works but is wrong" vs. "this is unconventional but correct"
3. Holistic System Awareness
Developers don't review code in isolation. The tool needs:
 
Cross-file and cross-service impact analysis
 
Understanding of runtime behavior, not just static syntax
 
Awareness of deployment constraints, performance characteristics, and failure modes
 
Knowledge of what isn't  in the codebase (missing tests, undocumented edge cases)
4. Judgment Under Uncertainty
Real developers make calls with incomplete information. The tool must:
 
Flag risks proportionally (not every code smell is critical)
 
Know when to suggest vs. when to ask for clarification
 
Balance idealism with pragmatism (perfect vs. shipped)
 
Recognize its own limitations and defer appropriately
